# Chroma library, modern Earth: LIFE EVENTS of love, family and friends, each open in every stage its Part D rates cover. Plan: earth-plan.md "More life events".
# Syntax: header of build.py. Each event is even on its own: alpha even, the five one-color options and its listed set. Rates: sources/life-events-and-everyday.md Part D.
# Audit 2026-10-05 (drafts/audit/BRIEF.md): options checked for realism and age; child versions (variant_of) follow the events they split. Changes: earth-life-ties-changes.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- falling in love (D02; pairs D2a)

== falling in love
tier: life event
stages: juvenile young_adult adult mature
age: 14-54
per_year: 0 .2 .4 .3 .1 0
drivers: ties+.5 prosper+.2 era+.2 health+.2 money+.2 stress-.3 harsh-.3 family-.2 fortune+.1
gap: 1-3
times: per single person a year: about 20 in 100 teenagers, 40 in 100 young adults, 30 in 100 adults and 10 in 100 in midlife begin a new serious relationship (D02 [est] gives 20, 25, 8 and 4 per person; set per eligible person by the engine at pull-in, 06:29, so that about seven in ten hold a partner at forty); from 55 new love comes through a late love (D12)
likelier: being single, a wide circle of friends, a move to a new town or a new job, a growing economy, dating apps
rarer: poor health, stress, caring for children or parents, a harsh or unsettled place, little money in the teens and twenties
excludes: partner
domain: partner1
rite: young_adult
tone: joy
life: love
horizon: months
roles: prospect, friend, sibling, parent
earth: someone you met through friends, and you cannot stop thinking about them
scene: The last bus has gone, and {N} and {prospect} are still talking on the bench outside the station, as if they have known each other for years.
scene W: {N} wants to do this properly: honest from the start, introduced to the people who matter, and meant.
scene U: {N} replays every conversation with {prospect} at night, sifting it for what it means and whether it could last.
scene B: {prospect} is clever, admired and plainly interested. {N} means to be the one who sets the pace.
scene R: Every time the phone buzzes, {Ns} heart jumps. It is ridiculous, and {N} has never felt so alive.
scene G: With {prospect}, {N} feels oddly at home, as if something has slid into place without anyone forcing it.
- W | promise to be faithful, and say plainly what you expect in return | v: conformity, security | tags: commit:partner | chance: 60
- U | ask them a long list of questions, to see whether you truly fit | v: self-direction, security | tags: commit:partner | chance: 48
- B | keep your own friends, money and plans, and let them fit in around you | v: self-direction, power | act: keep their own friends, money and plans, and let the new love fit in around them | tags: commit:partner | chance: 67
- R | say the three words first, before your nerve fails | v: stimulation | mark: took a wild risk | tags: commit:partner | chance: 58
- G | trust the feeling in your gut, and let it unfold in its own time | v: benevolence | tags: commit:partner | chance: 68
- WU | wait three months before calling it serious, and talk everything through | v: security, conformity | tags: commit:partner | chance: 64
- WG | bring them home to a family meal, and let the family take them in | door | v: tradition, benevolence | tags: commit:partner | chance: 62
- UR | set off on an adventure together, to see who they really are | door | v: stimulation, self-direction | tags: commit:partner | chance: 68
- BR | keep seeing other people on the quiet, and keep your options open | v: hedonism, self-direction | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: deceiving them; backfire: found out, and dropped | tags: commit:partner | chance: 48
- BG | make it plain to everyone that they are yours now, and guard it | v: power, security | act: make it plain to everyone that the new love is theirs now, and guard it | tags: commit:partner | chance: 50
works: {N} and {prospect} become a couple, and their friends say they saw it coming.
works: Months later, {N} still smiles at the sound of {prospect}'s name.
fails: After a few bright weeks, {prospect} stops answering, and {N} is left staring at the phone.
fails: It ends in a tearful argument on a street corner, and {N} walks home the long way.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- making peace with family you had cut off (D08; pairs D5b since 2026-10-07, the other half of
# 'a baby on the way', so pairs and triads stay even)

== making peace with family you had cut off
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-100
per_year: 0 0 .004 .004 .004 .004
drivers: ties+.4 community+.3 family+.3 health-.2 stress-.2
gap: 5-15
times: D08 [est]: about 1 in 100 teenagers and 2 in 100 people of every later age a year make peace with a sibling, a parent or a grown child they had cut off; about one American adult in four is cut off from a relative, and many later reconcile (Pillemer 2020); Part D's child figure (0.5 in 100) counts parents getting back together, a different event, so the child stage is left out; opens at 18 (audit 2026-10-05): a teenager still lives inside the family, and these acts (years of silence, the family money, limits on family occasions) are a grown person's, so D08's teenage 1 in 100 is left out too. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, 0, .004, .004, .004, .004 (child to elder): about 1 American adult in 4 is cut off from a relative (Pillemer 2020), and a share of them make peace; the old rate gave 7 in 10 lives.
likelier: friends and a community who keep both sides in touch, faith, a death or an illness in the family, growing older
rarer: family strife, a fight over money or an inheritance, great distance, pride on either side
tone: mixed
life: family
horizon: months
roles: sibling, parent, child, elder, a go-between
earth: after years of silence, the brother or sister you cut off asks to meet
scene: After six years of silence there is a message from {sibling}, three lines long, ending with a question: can we talk? {N} reads it in the hall with the coat still on.
scene W: {N} broke with {sibling} for a reason, and the reason still stands. But a family is meant to hold together, and {N} knows it.
scene U: {N} has told the story of the rift so often it has worn smooth. Reading {sibling}'s message, {N} wonders for the first time how {sibling} tells it.
scene B: {sibling} has written after all this time, and surely wants something. {N} would like to know what, and what it is worth.
scene R: {N} reads {sibling}'s message and is furious and close to tears at once, and has already half typed an answer.
scene G: As {elder} always says, a family comes back together in the end, the way water finds its level. {N} looks at {sibling}'s message for a long time.
- W | answer politely, and suggest one meeting on neutral ground | v: conformity, security | chance: 64
- U | ask them in writing what has changed, before agreeing to anything | v: security, self-direction | chance: 60
- B | make peace now, with one eye on the family money | v: power, security | chance: 60
- R | ring them that night and pour out everything held in for years | v: none clear (feelings let out) | chance: 60
- G | forgive them in front of the whole family, and let the old quarrel go | identity | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 62
- WU | write a letter owning your part, and post it the same night | v: benevolence | mark: owned up | chance: 76
- UG | ask an old aunt or a family friend to bring you together, and keep the peace | binds | v: tradition, security | act: ask an old aunt or a family friend to bring the two of them together, and keep the peace | chance: 65
- WB | agree to family occasions only, with clear limits, and nothing more | v: security, tradition | chance: 55
- BR | test them first with a small secret, and see whether it travels | v: security | chance: 38
- RG | turn up on their doorstep unannounced, and settle it face to face | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 60
works: The first meeting is stiff, the second easier, and by the third they are laughing at the same old jokes.
works: {N} and {sibling} sit up past midnight, and the years of silence begin to shrink.
fails: Within ten minutes they are back in the same old argument, word for word.
fails: {sibling} does not turn up, and the silence starts again, heavier than before.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- a parent dies (D51; pairs D2b)
# Audit 2026-10-05: split at 12. This version is for 12 and over; "losing a parent too soon" (variant_of) is the child's.

== a parent dies
tier: life event
stages: all
age: 12-100
per_year: .007 .007 .014 .035 .06 .03
drivers: money-.3 health-.2 harsh+.4 unrest+.3 stress+.2 era-.2 prosper-.2
times: D51 [data] for the young, [est] from the life table for later stages: about 0.4 in 100 children, 0.7 in 100 teenagers, 1.4 in 100 young adults, 3.5 in 100 adults, 6 in 100 in midlife and 3 in 100 elders a year lose a parent; the figures count a death of either parent; Part D's midlife 8 in 100 is per living parent and its elder 15 in 100 counts only those who still have a parent alive, so both are scaled here to everyone in the stage, until the engine can tell whether a parent is living; about 1 in 11 American children lose a parent or a sibling by 18; twice at most, more with step-parents; under 12 the event comes as "losing a parent too soon", which carries the child rate (audit 2026-10-05)
likelier: poverty, poor health in the family, a harsh or unsettled place, war, stress, older parents
rarer: money, good health and good health care, a prosperous and peaceful time
kills: parent
rite: mature
tone: trouble
life: loss, family
horizon: years
roles: parent, sibling, grandparent, partner, friend
earth: your mother or father dies
scene: The phone rings before dawn. It is {sibling}, and {N} knows from the first breath what has happened to {parent}.
scene W: {parent} has died, and there is the undertaker to call, the notice to write, the relatives to ring in the right order. {N} does each thing properly, because {parent} would have.
scene U: {N} reads {parent}'s hospital letters twice over, the dates, the doses, the last scan, looking for the moment it could have gone differently.
scene B: With {parent} gone, someone has to hold this family together and decide about the house, the money and the funeral. {N} already knows who.
scene R: The grief arrives like a wave and knocks {N} flat on the kitchen floor, sobbing for {parent} like a child.
scene G: {N} stands in {parent}'s garden among the plants {parent} put in decades ago. They will come up again in spring, and this time {N} will be the one to see them.
- W | keep every funeral custom, and stand beside the family the whole day | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 82
- U | ask every question about how and why they died, until it makes sense | v: self-direction | chance: 75
- B | decide nobody will ever see you cry, and look after yourself from now on | v: self-direction, security | act: decide nobody will ever see them cry, and look after themselves from now on | chance: 65
- R | cry and rage at everyone, and slam every door in the house | v: none clear (grief let out) | chance: 60
- G | plant something where they lie, and tend it every season | habit | v: tradition | chance: 72
- RG | go alone to their favourite place, and let the grief out where nobody can see | body: light | v: none clear (grief let out) | act: go alone to the lost parent's favourite place, and let the grief out where nobody can see | chance: 75
- UB | go through their things alone, looking for what they never told anyone | v: security, self-direction | chance: 70
- UG | make a book of everything you remember about them, before it fades | habit | v: tradition, benevolence | act: make a book of everything they remember about the parent they lost, before it fades | chance: 85
- WB | take on their part in the family, so it keeps running without them | v: security, benevolence | act: take on the lost parent's part in the family, so it keeps running without them | chance: 70
- WR | stand up at the funeral, voice shaking, and read out what you wrote for them | identity | v: benevolence, tradition | act: stand up at the funeral, voice shaking, and read out what they wrote for the parent they lost | chance: 85
works: In time {N} can talk about {parent} and laugh at the old stories.
works: The family pulls close in the weeks after the funeral, and {N} is not alone in it.
fails: {N} goes very quiet for a long time, and nobody quite knows how to reach {N}.
fails: The house feels wrong for months, and {N} keeps turning to tell {parent} things.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- losing a parent too soon (child version of "a parent dies"; pairs D2b, same codes in the same order)

== losing a parent too soon
tier: life event
variant_of: a parent dies
stages: child, juvenile
age: 3-12
per_year: .004 .007 0 0 0 0
drivers: money-.3 health-.2 harsh+.4 unrest+.3 stress+.2 era-.2 prosper-.2
times: the child slot of "a parent dies" (D51 [data]): about 0.4 in 100 children a year lose a parent, and about 1 in 11 American children lose a parent or a sibling by 18; the same event for once, kills and rates, told at a child's height; the juvenile slot (0.7 in 100) is the original's, so an early juvenile of 10 to 12 meets this version until 12
likelier: poverty, poor health in the family, a harsh or unsettled place, war, stress, older parents
rarer: money, good health and good health care, a prosperous and peaceful time
kills: parent
tone: trouble
life: loss, family
horizon: years
roles: parent, grandparent, sibling, friend
earth: your mother or father dies while you are still small
scene: {grandparent} kneels down in the hallway and holds both of {Ns} hands. Something has happened to {parent}, and the grown-ups in the kitchen have been crying.
scene W: There are good clothes laid out on the bed for {parent}'s funeral, and a lot of grown-ups in the front room speaking softly. {N} sits up very straight, the way {parent} would want.
scene U: {N} keeps asking where {parent} has gone, exactly, and why the doctors could not make it better. Nobody's answer quite fits.
scene B: Since {parent} died, everyone is busy and sad, and nobody is looking. {N} decides, quietly, to be someone who does not need anybody.
scene R: {N} does not want cake or hugs or quiet voices. {N} wants {parent} back, and shouts it into the pillow.
scene G: {parent} is gone, but the gardening gloves are still on the back step, in the shape of two hands. {N} sits beside them in the sun.
- W | put on your good clothes, and sit still and straight through the funeral | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 72
- U | ask the grown-ups where they have gone, again and again, until an answer fits | v: self-direction | act: ask the grown-ups where the lost parent has gone, again and again, until an answer fits | chance: 75
- B | decide not to need anybody, and look after yourself from now on | v: self-direction, security | act: decide not to need anybody, and look after themselves from now on | chance: 50
- R | scream into the pillow, and slam the door on anyone who tries to help | v: none clear (grief let out) | chance: 55
- G | help plant flowers by their grave, and water them on every visit | habit | v: tradition | act: help plant flowers by the lost parent's grave, and water them on every visit | chance: 72
- RG | run to the spot outdoors they loved best, and cry there as long as you need | body: light | v: none clear (grief let out) | act: run to the spot outdoors the lost parent loved best, and cry there as long as they need | chance: 68
- UB | creep into their room when nobody is looking, and go through their things | v: security, self-direction | act: creep into the lost parent's room when nobody is looking, and go through their things | chance: 72
- UG | make a memory box of their photos and treasures, with the family's help | habit | v: tradition, benevolence | act: make a memory box of the lost parent's photos and treasures, with the family's help | chance: 90
- WB | do little jobs around the house, so the family can manage without them | v: security, benevolence | chance: 75
- WR | say goodbye to them out loud at the funeral, in front of everyone | identity | v: benevolence, tradition | act: say goodbye to the lost parent out loud at the funeral, in front of everyone | chance: 72
works: In time {N} can look at the photographs of {parent} and smile.
works: {grandparent} and the family close in around {N}, and {N} is never left alone with it.
fails: {N} has bad dreams for months, and will not sleep without the landing light on.
fails: Nobody explains very much, and {N} is left alone with questions too big to carry.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- a brother or sister dies (D54; triads from D2b)

== a brother or sister dies
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-110
per_year: 0 0 .0025 .004 .012 .05
drivers: health-.3 money-.3 harsh+.4 unrest+.3 era-.2
times: D54 [est] (from the life table, about two siblings of near age): about 0.08 in 100 teenagers, 0.25 in 100 young adults, 0.4 in 100 adults, 1.2 in 100 in midlife and 5 in 100 elders a year lose a brother or sister; the figures count any of about two siblings; Part D's child figure (0.03 in 100) is left out, since a sibling's death in childhood is a child's death, which the plan sets aside with D53 until Emren decides; opens at 18 (audit 2026-10-05) for the same reason, since a teenager's brother or sister is mostly a child too, and these acts (the partner's wishes, settling their affairs, their family under your wing) are a grown person's
likelier: poor health in the family, poverty, a reckless brother or sister, a harsh or unsettled place, older siblings
rarer: money, good health and good health care
kills: sibling
tone: trouble
life: loss, family
horizon: years
roles: sibling, parent, partner, friend, the sibling's partner and children
earth: your brother or sister dies
scene: {N} is in the queue at the supermarket when the phone rings. {sibling} has died, and the basket in {Ns} hand suddenly weighs a ton.
scene W: {sibling} has died. There is a funeral to arrange and a family to stand by, and {N} straightens up and makes the first call.
scene U: {N} keeps going back over the last conversation with {sibling}, word by word, as if the answer were hidden somewhere in it.
scene B: {sibling} has left a house, debts and a family with nobody in charge. {N} sees at once who will have to take it all in hand.
scene R: When the call about {sibling} comes, {N} walks out into the rain without a coat, unable to stay indoors one second longer.
scene G: {N} and {sibling} grew up in the same small room, under the same roof, from the same roots. Half of {Ns} childhood has gone with {sibling}.
- W | insist on the funeral the family has always held, whatever their partner wanted | v: tradition, conformity | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: overruling the widow or widower; backfire: a row at the graveside, and the partner's side of the family lost for good | chance: 55
- U | trace the family's health history, to learn what took them | v: security, self-direction | chance: 65
- B | get straight back to work or school, and let nobody see how hard it hit | habit | v: achievement, self-direction | chance: 65
- R | take up the plans they never got to, and live them out | binds | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 70
- G | go back alone to where you grew up together, and walk the old paths | body: light | v: tradition | chance: 78
- WUB | settle their affairs line by line, so nobody can cheat their family | binds | v: security, benevolence | chance: 75
- WRG | ask that the next baby born in the family carry their name | v: tradition | chance: 45
- WBR | take their family under your wing, and see they lack nothing | binds | v: benevolence, power | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 78
- URG | hold the wake your own way, in the woods they loved, with their music | identity | v: hedonism, tradition | chance: 65
- UBG | keep their secrets, and burn the letters nobody else should read | v: benevolence, security | chance: 72
works: The family gathers around {sibling}'s memory, and the old childhood jokes come out at the wake.
works: A year on, {N} visits the grave and finds the grief has softened into something {N} can carry.
fails: The funeral opens old wounds, and two branches of the family stop speaking.
fails: {N} cannot bear to say {sibling}'s name for years, and the silence costs something.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- you take in a child who needs a home (D05; triads from D3a)

== you take in a child who needs a home
tier: life event
at: ministry
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 20-85
per_year: 0 0 .0005 .001 .0008 .0003
drivers: community+.4 money+.3 health+.2 unrest-.3
times: D05 [est]: about 5 in 10,000 young adults, 10 in 10,000 adults, 8 in 10,000 in midlife and 3 in 10,000 elders a year adopt, foster or take in a child, often a grandchild, a niece or a nephew whose parents cannot cope; it leans on about 100,000 to 120,000 US adoptions a year, step-parent and foster adoptions included
likelier: a family crisis among kin, longing for a child that has not come, a church or a close community, enough money
rarer: low money, poor health, war and unrest (which close international adoption)
domain: children1
rite: adult
tone: joy
life: children, family
horizon: years
roles: child, partner, sibling, parent, a social worker
earth: the agency rings: a child in the family needs a home, tonight if possible
scene: A small rucksack, a toothbrush in a plastic bag, and a child of seven in the doorway, looking at everything and touching nothing. The social worker says it may be for a week, or for good.
scene W: {N} said yes on the phone without a second thought. A child needed a home, and that is what a family is for.
scene U: {N} has read the file twice: the school reports, the doctor's notes, the gaps between them. Understanding this child is going to take time.
scene B: {N} has wanted a child for years, and the usual way never came. Now here is one, and {N} does not mean to let the chance slip.
scene R: {N} sees the small face in the doorway and something in {Ns} chest simply opens. Of course the child is staying.
scene G: {N} puts a jar of flowers from the garden by the bed made up for the child who came today. A child is like a seedling: it needs ground, water and time.
- W | do it by the book: the checks, the forms, the court, the promise | v: conformity, benevolence | tags: commit:children | chance: 72
- U | foster first, to see whether it works for everyone before deciding | v: security | tags: commit:children | chance: 68
- B | teach them from the first day never to be anyone's doormat | v: self-direction, power | tags: commit:children | chance: 62
- R | say yes on the spot, and make up the spare bed that very night | binds | v: benevolence, stimulation | mark: helped someone in need | tags: commit:children | chance: 65
- G | take them in the old way, as family, with no papers and no fuss | binds | v: tradition, benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | closed: law: no legal standing; backfire: no right to sign school or doctor's forms, and a long fight in court later | tags: commit:children | law: childminding | chance: 72
- WUR | learn every way to win a wary child's trust, and practise them daily | habit | v: benevolence, achievement | mark: learned a skill | tags: commit:children | chance: 80
- WUB | draw up a plan: rules, routines, and a savings account in their name | binds | v: security | tags: commit:children | chance: 70
- WRG | throw a welcome party with the whole family, so they know they belong | door | v: benevolence, tradition | tags: commit:children | chance: 60
- UBG | dig into the birth family's past, and keep what you find to yourself | v: security | tags: commit:children | chance: 55
- BRG | take on the school, the agency, anyone who stands in their way | identity | v: benevolence, power | tags: commit:children | chance: 65
works: Within a year the child laughs at dinner and leaves muddy shoes in the hall like any other child.
works: The court makes it permanent, and the family throws a party nobody will forget.
fails: The child keeps {N} at arm's length for months, and every evening is hard work.
fails: The placement breaks down after a hard winter, and the child moves on to another home.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- a partner's affair comes to light (D45; pairs D3b)

== a partner's affair comes to light
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-90
per_year: 0 0 .0088 .007 .0053 .0018
drivers: stress+.5 money+.2 era+.2 community-.4 trouble+.1
gap: 3-10
times: D45 [data] for the share who stray, [est] for the half found out: per person in a couple, about 2.5 in 100 young adults, 2 in 100 adults, 1.5 in 100 in midlife and 0.5 in 100 elders a year find out that their partner has been unfaithful; about 1 in 5 married American men and 1 in 8 women report ever straying (General Social Survey), and perhaps half of affairs are found out. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, 0, .0088, .007, .0053, .0018 (child to elder): about 2 in 10 married men and 1 in 8 married women in the US report an affair (GSS), and about half come to light; the old rate gave 6 in 10 lives.
likelier: stress, long separations for work or war, an unhappy marriage, dating apps, money and the chances it brings
rarer: a close community, religion, a close marriage
needs: partner
domain: partner1
tone: trouble
life: love, conflict
horizon: months
roles: partner, friend, sibling, parent, a lawyer
earth: a message lights up on your partner's phone, and it is not from a colleague
scene: {partner}'s phone lights up on the kitchen table while {partner} is out of the room: a name {N} does not know, and a message no colleague would send.
scene W: {N} made a vow, and so did {partner}. Only one of them has kept it.
scene U: {N} reads the message three times, then goes back through the months: the late meetings, the new password, the trip that changed dates.
scene B: {N} sits very still with {partner}'s phone and the message on it. Before anyone knows what {N} knows, there is time to make it count.
scene R: {N} reads the message on {partner}'s phone, and the cup hits the wall before {N} has even decided to throw it.
scene G: One message on {partner}'s phone, and the house, the years, the photographs on the stairs all look different. {N} feels the ground of a whole life shift underfoot.
- W | tell both families exactly what they did, and let everyone judge | identity | v: conformity | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: shaming a partner before the family; backfire: the families take sides, and the gossip runs both ways | chance: 42
- U | say nothing for weeks, and read their messages to learn everything | v: security | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: spying on a partner; backfire: caught at it, and now both of them have something to answer for | tech: phone | chance: 62
- B | sort out your own money, home and work first, then decide about them | v: self-direction, security | chance: 70
- R | throw their things out of the window into the street | body: light | v: none clear (rage let out) | chance: 28
- G | turn the whole family against them, and shut them out for good | v: tradition, security | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: shunning; backfire: the family splits, and some take the other side | chance: 42
- BR | pack a bag that night, walk out, and let them do the chasing | v: self-direction | mark: left home | chance: 60
- UG | ask what was missing between you, and try counselling together | binds | v: benevolence, security | chance: 60
- UR | ask every question, there and then, until you understand what happened | v: self-direction | chance: 62
- WB | see a lawyer before saying a word, and get everything in writing | v: security, power | chance: 78
- WG | stay, for the vows and the family, and never speak of it again | binds | v: tradition, conformity | chance: 35
works: The whole truth comes out, and {N} decides about the future with clear eyes.
works: It is the hardest year of {Ns} life, and {N} comes through it standing.
fails: The arguments go on for months, and the whole family is dragged into them.
fails: {N} cannot stop going over it, and sleeps badly for weeks.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- betrayed by a friend or a business partner (D63; triads from D3b)

== betrayed by a friend or a business partner
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-100
per_year: 0 0 .02 .012 .008 .004
drivers: money+.2 stress+.3 unrest+.3 community-.4 ties-.2 family-.2 trouble+.1
gap: 3-8
times: D63 [est]: about 5 in 100 young adults, 3 in 100 adults, 2 in 100 in midlife and 1 in 100 elders a year are badly betrayed by a friend or a business partner; Part D's child and teen figures (5 and 10 in 100) count schoolyard betrayals such as a told secret, which batch 1's "a friend betrays your trust" and the juvenile file's secret about a friend already carry, so this grown-up event, with money or a living at stake, starts at eighteen. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, 0, .02, .012, .008, .004 (child to elder): about half of people are badly let down by a friend or partner in business at some point (estimate; the old rate gave 85 in 100 lives two each).
likelier: money at stake, stress, competition, unrest, a loose community where a good name counts for little
rarer: a close community where names matter, wide ties, a close family
