times: D74 [data] (BJS 2023): about 2 in 100 teenagers, 4 in 100 people aged 18 to 24, 2.3 in 100 adults, 2 in 100 in midlife and 0.8 in 100 elders a year are robbed or assaulted, about twice as often in cities and among the poor; opens at 16, because Emren's limits keep harm to children out of play; Part D's child figure (1 in 100) counts school fights, which the engine's playground dispute and the juvenile file's fight after school already hold. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, .0095, .018, .01, .009, .0036 (child to elder): about 4 in 10 violent crimes are by strangers (BJS); the yearly figure for all violent crime gave two a life.
likelier: youth, poverty, city streets, unrest, drinking, having been a victim before, being a man (for attacks by strangers), being openly gay or trans where people frown on it
rarer: money, country life, marriage, a close community, age
tone: trouble
life: community, body
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, partner, sibling, the police
earth: on the way home, two strangers corner you and take your phone and your wallet
scene: On the walk home under the railway bridge, two strangers block the path. One shove, a hand in {Ns} pocket, and they are gone at a run with the phone and the wallet.
scene W: The two who took {Ns} phone and wallet are gone. There are laws, there are police, there are cameras on that bridge. {N} stands shaking under the streetlight and asks a passer-by to ring the police.
scene U: The two robbers: one tall, in a grey hood; one with a limp; both heading east. {N} repeats the details aloud, so as not to lose them.
scene B: Someone took something from {N} tonight and got away with it. {N} will not be someone people take from.
scene R: Two strangers shove {N}, grab the phone and run. The fear lasts a second; then comes the fury, hot and huge, and {Ns} legs want to run after them.
scene G: After the robbery {N} walks home the long way, through the lit streets, and the town feels like a different and wilder place.
- W | report it at once, give a full statement, and see it through to court | identity | v: security, conformity | chance: 45
- U | study the streets and change your routes, so it cannot happen again | habit | v: security | chance: 68
- B | make yourself a hard target: never alone, never lost, never soft | habit | v: power, security | chance: 75
- R | carry a knife from now on, and let anyone try it again | v: security, power | closed: law: carrying a weapon; backfire: arrested at a stop and search, and a record to follow | law: knives | chance: 38
- G | stop going out after dark, and keep close to home and your own | v: security, tradition | mark: stayed home | chance: 25
- W>U | photograph and report every stranger who hangs about near that bridge | v: security, conformity | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: suspecting strangers on sight; backfire: an innocent neighbour reported, and the street turns cold | chance: 25
- U>B | track the stolen phone yourself, and find out exactly where they live | v: power, security | tech: phone | chance: 35
- B>R | find them later, with friends, and pay them back in kind | body: heavy | v: power | mark: made an enemy | closed: law: assault; backfire: arrested, and they come back with more friends | chance: 15
- R>G | take up boxing, and get strong enough that nobody tries it again | habit | body: heavy | v: security, achievement | chance: 68
- G>W | let the family deal with them in their own way, and keep the police out | v: tradition, security | mark: defied an authority | closed: law: taking justice into the family's hands; backfire: a feud, and arrests on both sides | chance: 30
works: The police find the phone, and one of the two is caught within the month.
works: The fear fades slowly, and by spring {N} walks home the old way without thinking about it.
fails: Nobody is ever caught, and {N} flinches at footsteps for a long time.
fails: {N} stops going out after dark, and the evenings shrink.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- your child's big success (D11; cycle s4)

== your child's big success
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 25-100
per_year: 0 0 .002 .012 .06 .04
drivers: money+.3 family+.3 community+.3 health+.2 unrest-.3
gap: 4-10
times: D11 [est]: about 0.5 in 100 young parents, 3 in 100 parents in the adult years, 15 in 100 in midlife and 10 in 100 elders a year see a child graduate, marry, land the job they wanted, win a prize or make a name; it leans on the timing of children's graduations, weddings and first jobs. Point 12 (Emren 2026-10-05): the yearly figures above are lowered to 0, 0, .002, .012, .06, .04 (child to elder): about one big success per child, about two per parent (estimate; the old rate gave four).
likelier: money, a close family, education, a community that celebrates its young
rarer: family strife, poor health, unrest
needs: children
domain: children1
tone: joy
life: children, family
horizon: week
roles: child, partner, grandparent, friend
earth: your son or daughter graduates, wins the prize, or gets the job they dreamed of
scene: {child} rings at lunchtime, too excited to make sense at first: the place, the prize, the job, the thing worked for over years, is theirs.
scene W: {N} sits in the hall in a good suit as {child} crosses the stage. Years of checked homework, kept rules and early nights, and here it is.
scene U: As the news about {child} sinks in, {N} remembers {child} at six, taking the radio apart on the kitchen floor. {N} always knew that mind would find its way.
scene B: {child} has come top of the year. {N} wants every relative, neighbour and rival to know exactly whose child that is.
scene R: {N} hears the news and lets out a whoop that sets the dog barking, then rings everyone at once.
scene G: {N} watches {child} walk out into the world, as {N} once did, and {Ns} own parents before. The tree has put out a new branch.
- W | make a proper speech at the family dinner, and give them a gift to keep | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 82
- U | ask what they want to do with it, and help them plan the next step | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 82
- B | boast about it everywhere, as if it were your own success | v: power, achievement | chance: 68
- R | throw a huge party and tell everyone you have ever met | v: hedonism | chance: 90
- G | step back, and let them make their own way from here | v: benevolence | chance: 72
- W>G | hang the photograph on the family wall, beside the grandparents' | v: tradition | chance: 94
- U>W | go through the contract and the small print with them, line by line | v: security, benevolence | chance: 75
- B>U | ring your contacts to open the next door for them | v: achievement, power | chance: 78
- R>B | push them, while the fire is lit, to aim even higher | v: achievement, stimulation | chance: 55
- G>R | take them off somewhere wild for a weekend, just the two of you | body: light | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 78
works: The whole family gathers to celebrate, and {child} gives a speech that makes {N} cry.
works: {child} rings on the first day of the new life, just to say thanks.
fails: The celebration turns sour when an old family argument comes back at the table.
fails: {child} rushes off into the new life, and the phone calls grow rare.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- your closest friend dies (D55; cycle s4)

== your closest friend dies
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-110
per_year: 0 0 .003 .005 .015 .04
drivers: health-.3 harsh+.4 unrest+.3 money-.3 era-.2 prosper-.2
gap: 5-10
times: D55 [est] (from the life table): Part D's 0.6 in 100 young adults, 1 in 100 adults, 3 in 100 in midlife and 10 in 100 elders a year count any of about five close friends; the closest one is set here at about two fifths of that (0.3, 0.5, 1.5 and 4 in 100); the elder stage's memorial service for a friend holds the other losses; Part D's child and teen figures are left out, since a friend of the same age dying before eighteen is a child's death, which the plan sets aside with D53 until Emren decides
likelier: age, a reckless youth, poor health, unrest, a harsh place, poverty
rarer: good health, modern medicine, a prosperous time
kills: friend
rite: elder
tone: trouble
life: loss, friends
horizon: years
roles: friend, partner, colleague, the friend's family
earth: the friend you have known longest dies
scene: {friend}'s number is still at the top of {Ns} phone, the last message unanswered. The funeral is on Thursday, and {N} has been asked to say a few words.
scene W: {friend} has died, and the family will need help with the arrangements, the house, the long months after. {N} knows what a friend owes, and means to pay it.
scene U: Since {friend} died, {N} keeps reaching for details that are already slipping: the band they saw at nineteen, the joke about the broken tent. They must be written down.
scene B: {friend} was the same age, near enough. {N} looks in the mirror and counts the years that might be left.
scene R: {N} reads the message from {friend}'s family, throws the phone onto the sofa, and howls like a child.
scene G: {friend} is gone, but the oak they planted together years ago, for a laugh, is taller than both of them now. {N} goes and sits under it.
- W | go to every memorial and anniversary for them, and never miss one | habit | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 75
- U | promise their family a book of their life, and write it chapter by chapter | binds | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 60
- B | decide life is too short, and start taking what you want from it | identity | v: hedonism, power | chance: 65
- R | speak at the funeral, and promise out loud to live the way they did | binds | identity | v: benevolence, stimulation | chance: 85
- G | keep to the few friends you have left, and let nobody new in | v: security, tradition | mark: turned down a chance | chance: 35
- W>G | stand by their family for years: birthdays, repairs, the bad days | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 72
- U>W | plan the memorial yourself, every reading and song exactly as they would want | identity | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 80
- B>U | step into their place on the project, and finish it as well as they would have | v: achievement | chance: 70
- R>B | hand in your notice that week, and chase the life they always told you to | v: self-direction, achievement | chance: 50
- G>R | carry their ashes up the hill they loved, whatever their family says | identity | body: heavy | v: tradition, stimulation | closed: approval: going against the family's wishes; backfire: a bitter row, and the friend's family never speaks to the person again | chance: 72
works: At the memorial the room is full, and {N} tells a story about {friend} that has everyone laughing through their tears.
works: {N} keeps {friend}'s memory close, and in time the grief makes room for gratitude.
fails: {N} withdraws for a long winter, and other friendships wither too.
fails: The funeral passes in a blur, and afterwards the silence where {friend} used to be is enormous.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- added 2026-10-05 06:40 at the engine's pull-in (fix 4)
# A grandparent's death after childhood (the child stage's "a grandparent dies" is told at a child's height). Pairs D4a.

== losing a grandparent
tier: life event
stages: juvenile young_adult adult
age: 12-50
per_year: 0 .05 .06 .04 0 0
drivers: harsh+.4 health-.3 money-.2
times: the engine rates deaths by the grandparent's own age (pull-in, 06:29); for scale, about 5 in 100 teenagers, 6 in 100 young adults and 4 in 100 adults a year lose a grandparent (estimate from the life table, as in Part D6); the child stage has its own version, told at a child's height ("a grandparent dies", earth-child.lib); opens at 12 (audit 2026-10-05), and the options are worded so a teenager can do them too
likelier: older grandparents, poverty, a harsh life, illness in the family
rarer: young and healthy grandparents, good health care
kills: grandparent
tone: trouble
life: loss, family
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, parent, sibling, partner
earth: your grandmother or grandfather dies
scene: The message comes on an ordinary afternoon: {grandparent} has died, quietly, in the night.
scene W: There is a funeral to arrange and a family to gather, and {parent} suddenly looks older. {N} knows what a grandchild ought to do now.
scene U: {N} realises how little anyone knows about {grandparent}'s life before the family: the years, the places, the reasons.
scene B: {grandparent} leaves a house, a little money and a family already eyeing both. {N} notices who is taking charge.
scene R: {N} had meant to visit last month. The grief hits all at once, hot and sharp, on the bus home.
scene G: The kitchen still smells of {grandparent}'s cooking. Outside, the plants {grandparent} put in are flowering as if nothing has happened.
- W | be there for the funeral, and help the family with whatever needs doing | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 88
- U | sit with the old letters and photographs, and write down the family's story | habit | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 85
- B | help clear the house, and ask for the keepsake in return | v: power (resources) | chance: 75
- R | drop everything and go at once, to see them before the coffin is closed | body: light | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- G | take on the old recipes and the garden, and keep them going as they did | habit | binds | v: tradition | chance: 82
- BG | argue hard against selling the old house, so it stays in the family | v: tradition, power (resources) | chance: 40
- RG | walk their old walk alone, all day, until the grief settles | body: light | v: none clear (grief let out) | chance: 82
- UR | track down their oldest friends and ask for every story, the wilder the better | v: self-direction, stimulation | chance: 70
- WB | step up and organise what needs doing, since nobody else will | identity | v: power, conformity | chance: 75
- WU | prepare a few careful words for the funeral, telling their life in order | identity | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 88
works: The family gathers, tells the old stories, and goes home closer than it came.
works: {N} carries something of {grandparent} forward, and it feels right.
fails: The funeral is tense, and old quarrels in the family open up again.
fails: {N} keeps busy and does not grieve, and it catches up months later.
